// The negative half of the sealed-fn rule: a named `fn` cannot *read* a top-level binding either.
// `use (…)` is the only door, in both directions, and it is a static error to walk past it.
//
// The rule is load-bearing well beyond the diagnostic. It is what makes a top-level binding
// reachable from a bounded, enumerable set of places — the top level itself, plus whatever names it
// in a `use (…)` list or captures in a closure — which is what lets the compiler decide where to
// store one.
// expect: error E0005 at 12:12
// expect: exit 1
mut total = 7;
fn read(): int {
    return total;
}
echo read();
